    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. When you mention demographic shifts and \"local community profile\" changes, can you be more specific? Like, are there certain types of shifts in the makeup of a neighborhood—socially or culturally speaking—that the data typically shows are more closely linked to an increase in default risk or economic instability?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Rapid increases in income inequality or significant changes in the socioeconomic makeup of the resident population can be red flags for instability. High rates of short-term renter populations, contrasted with established homeowners, often indicate transient communities less invested in long-term local commerce. Sudden influxes of residents with dramatically different employment profiles compared to the existing local economy can strain existing local infrastructure and demand. Therefore, look for uniformity in stability rather than drastic sudden changes to those established patterns.",
      "source": "target",
